  "query_preview": "..\n(Monday night through Saturday)\nIssued at 1231 AM CDT Sun Nov 1 2026\n\nThe parent storm system across the Intermountain West is then \nforecast to advance into the Central Plains from Monday night into \nTuesday. This will sweep a cold front across the region overnight \nthrough Tuesday morning and should exit the Coastal Plain counties \nsometime early afternoon. A line of showers and storms (could be \nbroken) will be expected along the front. There should be sufficient \ndeep layer shear around 30 to 40 kt range and enough instability \ndespite the time of day, between 500 and 1000 J/kt to support the \npossibility for a few isolated strong to severe storms within that \nline. The primary severe hazard would be damaging winds but some \nlarge hail may be possible as well. A level 1 of 5 risk for severe \nweather is highlighted for areas along and north/west of the I-35 \ncorridor in the outlook ending at 12Z Tuesday. I would not be \nsurprised if SPC does expand this risk to include areas farther to \nthe east in the next update for the outlook that ends at 12Z on \nWednesday. The rain chances will quickly end behind the front as a \nnorthwesterly flow establishes and ushers in drier air across the \nregion. Wind speeds increase but not like some of our stronger \nfronts in the cool season. \n\nThe drier airmass results in cooler mornings during mid to late in \nthe week where the Hill Country dips into the 40s while elsewhere \nareas generally fall into the low to mid 50s. Afternoons are to \nremain mild but low humidity should keep the weather pleasant with \nmostly sunny skies and no rain. The weather becomes more unclear \nheading through next weekend with differences within the medium \nrange guidance on the location and progression of various upper \nlevel features. The latest NBM continues to have low (10-20%) rain \nchances entering into the picture as of now with some low-level \nmoisture.",
